---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ImageZoom from '../../../components/ImageZoom.astro';
import '../../../styles/prose.css';
import styles from './mission-control.module.css';
import roster from '../../../assets/projects/mission-control/roster.svg';
import detail from '../../../assets/projects/mission-control/detail.svg';

const repo = 'https://github.com/StanKarz/mission-control';

const description =
  'A terminal dashboard for tracking several Claude Code projects at once, where the progress bar is computed from predicates rather than ticked by hand.';
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission control" description={description} wide>
  <article class={styles.page}>
    <header class={styles.header}>
      <p class={styles.kicker}><a href="/projects/">projects</a> / mission control</p>
      <h1 class={styles.title}>Mission control</h1>
      <p class={styles.lede}>
        A terminal dashboard for keeping track of several projects at once. One row each: how far
        through it is, what the next unmet goal is, when it was last touched, and whether a Claude
        Code session is running in it right now.
      </p>
      <p class={styles.meta}>
        Python, <a href="https://textual.textualize.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Textual</a
        >, 112 tests. <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I had five projects on the go and nowhere that tracked where any of them stood. Opening one
        meant reading the last commit, trying to remember what "done" was supposed to mean, and
        losing ten minutes before any work started. The ones I had not touched in a fortnight
        quietly stopped existing.
      </p>

      <figure class={styles.figure}>
        <img src={roster.src} alt="The roster: one row per project, each with a status glyph, a progress bar, a week of session activity, and when it was last touched. The highlighted row expands to show its next unmet check and last commit." />
        <figcaption>
          The highlighted row expands in place. Choosing what to pick up needs the next action far
          more than it needs a percentage.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Progress you can't fake</h2>

      <p>
        A hand-ticked checklist rots. You tick three boxes in week one, never open it again, and the
        percentage becomes a lie that is worse than no number. So every item that <em>can</em> be a
        predicate the app evaluates on open is one:
      </p>

      <pre><code>{`[[projects."orbital-sim".checks]]
name  = "eval harness green"
type  = "cmd"                       # passes on exit 0
value = "pytest -q tests/test_eval.py"

[[projects."orbital-sim".checks]]
name  = "baseline run"
type  = "path"                      # file exists
value = "results/baseline.json"

[[projects."orbital-sim".checks]]
name  = "picked the reward shaping"
type  = "manual"                    # nothing can measure this one
done  = false`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Five types: <code>path</code>, <code>git_tag</code>, <code>cmd</code>, <code>gh_pr</code>,
        and <code>manual</code> for the rest. Progress is passing divided by total, recomputed every
        time, so it cannot drift. A project with no checks shows a dash, never 0%.
      </p>

      <p>
        The upstream benefit is the real one: it forces you to say what done looks like. "Writeup"
        is not a check. "<code>writeup.md</code> exists and is over 800 words" is.
      </p>

      <figure class={styles.figure}>
        <img src={detail.src} alt="The detail screen for one project: a large percentage, the full check list with ticks and unresolved circles, a week of commits and sessions as heatmaps, recent commits, and recent session titles." />
        <figcaption>
          One project up close. Cheap checks run on open; the ones that shell out or hit the network
          are cached, and marked as unresolved until you run them.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The rest of it</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Sends <code>cd &lt;path&gt; &amp;&amp; claude --resume &lt;id&gt;</code> to the tmux pane next door,
          and refuses rather than typing it into a program already running there.
        </li>
        <li>
          Shows which projects have a session running, and which of those have finished and are
          waiting on you. Useful during a long agent run.
        </li>
        <li>
          Rolls the week or the month up per project, from commit messages and the titles Claude
          writes for its own sessions. Nothing is summarised by a model.
        </li>
        <li>
          Repairs sessions that lost their project. Claude Code names each session directory after
          the absolute path you launched from, so moving a project silently breaks
          <code>--resume</code>. There is no built-in repair, and everything that mutates is a dry
          run until you pass <code>--go</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Try it</h2>

      <pre><code>{`uv tool install git+https://github.com/StanKarz/mission-control
mc init          # writes a starter config
mc doctor        # finds every directory with Claude sessions`}</code></pre>

      <p>
        Requires Python 3.12 or newer. tmux is optional and only the launcher needs it. The code,
        a longer guide and the gotchas are
        <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">on GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <ImageZoom />
</BaseLayout>
